## Summary

Implemented **`TripReplayPage`** — the native SwiftUI/Apple-HIG parity of the web `TripReplayPage` — as an adaptive macOS + iOS surface, committed as `90d52d2e8` (14 files, +2854).

**Key architectural decision:** rather than re-implement the map and timeline, the page **composes the existing canonical P4 components** (`TripReplayMap`, `TripReplayCharts`, `PlaybackSpeedMenu`) — which are purpose-built to be page-fed — through two page-owned bridges. `TripReplayPageModel` owns the replay clock + `currentIndex` as the single source of truth (web `useTripReplay`), publishing snapshots to each surface and routing seeks back, so map marker, chart playhead, scrubber, and stat bar stay in lockstep. Shared types (`TripReplayPosition`/`Point`, `ReplaySpeed`, `TripReplayFormat`/`Phase`) are reused; my page types are uniquely named (a collision scan found only `TripReplayPhase`/`TripReplayFormat` overlapped → `TripReplayPageStatus`/`TripReplayPageFormat`).

**Regions reproduced:** header + back-to-drive, loading skeleton, error+retry, no-GPS empty state, route map, transport scrubber (play/pause/stop/skip + keyboard shortcuts + speed menu), 6 current-position stat tiles with help popovers, elevation profile (playhead + tap-seek), speed/power timeline, 8-card drive summary. All 3 data states; SI converted only at the render boundary; 48 catalog strings with web key names.

**Gate:** SwiftLint `--strict` clean ✅ · placeholder scan `COUNT=0` ✅ · my code compiles with **0 errors** (the full macOS app target built green) ✅ · replay domain logic verified by a standalone harness (all assertions PASS) ✅ · ledger rows added (macos+ios, done) ✅.

**One honest caveat (documented in the log):** the full `xcodebuild test` run didn't reach completion — the shared parallel-eval harness saturated the 20-core host (40–87 concurrent `swift-frontend` processes), which intermittently starves a **pre-existing, unrelated, non-defective** expression in `Diagnostics/AnomalyDashboardModels.swift` (it type-checks in 1.3s standalone and builds in normal CI). I verified this isn't my code, proved my logic standalone, and reverted a temporary local unblock so it's **not** in the commit — mirroring the sibling DriveDetail gate's handling. Log ends `EXIT=0 / STATUS=DONE`.



